"coach.trendMetric" = "CHANGE / ROUND";
"coach.readingTitle" = "HOW IT IS GOING";
"coach.pcgTrendMetric" = "CHANGE / ROUND";
"coach.summary.info" = "The trend is your last three rounds against the average of every round read here — the last seven, and only rounds that were finished.\n\nIt takes half a stroke a round either way before a direction is called; anything smaller is the same putting on different days.\n\nWhen nothing is moving, the wording depends on where you are standing still: above +0.5 a round is a good place to stand, below −0.5 is not.";
"coach.numbers.info" = "The first two tiles show change, not level: your last three rounds against the average of your last seven. Green is ground made, red is ground lost, whatever the size.\n\nThe small Ø line underneath is that average itself — where the change is measured from. What your putting is worth overall belongs in the Statistics tab; here the question is which way it is moving.\n\nSG is strokes gained over a whole round: for every hole, the putts the tour would expect from where the first putt stood, less the putts you took. PCG is the same idea per putt, against the tour's odds of holing from that distance.\n\n3-putts are counted per round rather than as a share of holes. The last tile is the distance band costing you the most: the band, then the conversion gain lost there against the tour.\n\nAll of it is read from your finished rounds on the course. The drills are counted separately, further down.";
"coach.practice.info" = "Read from the completed drills in the Games tab, and kept apart from the course numbers on purpose: practice without a card in your hand is not a round, and averaging the two would describe neither.\n\nPCG per putt appears for the drills that record the distance of each attempt; the ones counted on the green without the app do not have one.";
"coach.whatIsHappening.info" = "Habits in your misses: a side they keep going, a length they keep coming up, a break they keep missing below.\n\nEach line needs at least eight misses of that kind (six within a slice), with more than six in ten going the same way, before it is called a habit. One bad putt is a bad putt.\n\nNeeds the complex result field: Pro mode, or Custom with the result set to complex.\n\nWhere one miss reason stands out behind a habit, it is named underneath. The lines with a link symbol tie reasons to directions and kinds of putts. For misreads and wrong aims it also says how the break was read. A miss only counts as long when the next putt is more than 1 m: a ball that stops just past the hole had the right pace. Two exceptions are named from 30 percent: misses inside 3 m that come up short, and putts from 8 m that finish more than 1 m from the hole. The findings are grouped into length, line and break, and distance left. Each group shows at most three, one per direction, and the groups and the findings in them are ordered by roughly how many putts they cost.";
"coach.conditions.info" = "Every condition a round carries — the pace of the greens, rain, wind, cold, grain — and every condition a putt carries — break, slope, double break — is compared with its opposite over your own putts.\n\nA difference is only listed when it survives four checks: enough putts on both sides, both sides putting from comparable distances, a statistical test that the two shares really differ, and a correction for how many comparisons were made to find it. Search enough splits and random data throws up differences on its own; this is what keeps those off the card.\n\nThese are read from your last thirty rounds rather than ten. How you putt in the rain is a trait rather than a form, and rain is rare.";
"coach.plan.info" = "In order: the distance costing you the most strokes, the drill that answers whatever your misses keep doing, the drill you convert worst in, and whatever has been left untouched for two weeks.\n\nThe number beside each drill is how many sessions to aim for before the next read — three when the trend is slipping, one when it is climbing, two otherwise.";

